bout the  importance of young diverse people  seeing those who look like them  represented in tv and film and at these  award shows  is it not a little bit of a shame for  for her to win when it couldn't be seen  when it couldn't be televised  um well i think on your first point  representation is huge it matters  incredibly i mean if you look online and  google you know little kids with their  faces next to the encanto screen um for  finally getting to see someone that  looks like them on screen i mean it's  it's beautiful um and i think that you  know  uh the movies and tv that came out last  year just really  moved the needle forward in terms of  representation um and so far is her not  being able to give a speech yeah i mean  it's  i wish it was under different  circumstances but i'm just really really  glad um that you know this historic  moment happened  and kimberly last year emily in paris  was nominated and won but i may destroy  you was ignored and there was a lot of  outrage over that it led to the la times  uh doing a complete investigation into  the hollywood foreign press association  and the inner workings  how what was your big takeaway from that  reporting and how did that influence  your decision to join the association  it was probably the biggest reason i  joined if i'm being honest um i was  really appalled that it wasn't nominated  to me one of the most creative  tv shows to come along in decades um and  so i just thought i can continue to bark  from the outside or i can try to change  an organization from within and that's  what i feel like i've been doing  and tell us a bit about that process  kimberly i know that you're based in  ireland do you cover the u.s  entertainment industry from there  um well i am back and forth between new  york san francisco and ireland all the  time um i write predominantly for film  ireland but um you know i've been a  german entertainment journalist for like  20 years um so you know i write for uh  very like different irish papers and um  i mean i don't even know where to begin  just lots of different places but mainly  from ireland  got it got it now the hollywood foreign  press association announced a whole new  string of bylaws that you had to agree  to any new member had to agree to when  they joined the organization to address  the ethics issues that uh and diversity  issues that they were called out for can  you walk us through some of those um  bylaws and whether they go far enough in  your view  um well i can't specifically speak to  the bylaws themselves um but i can say  that they are some of the strictest  things i've signed i've been in this  industry for a very long time and um  they are sweeping um ethical like  changes to the organization so i think  there's very little room for members to  have any sort of um there shouldn't be  anything suspects happening i'll put it  that way yeah and we were able to have  the chief diversity officer from the  hfpa on our program a little while ago  and we talked about how hfpa still has  not met its own membership goal of 13  black membership what do you see  kimberly that convinces you that things  are going to be different moving forward  what's the biggest thing that to you  says okay they're going to meet this  goal in 2022  um well i think the application process  you know i know for a fact that um  people who applied last year who maybe  didn't get in were given extra training  on how they can better prepare their  applications for um this coming year  which is something that not a lot of  organizations do um and so i think the  organization from what i can tell what  i've seen since i joined  is  dead set on change and moving forward  and moving the needle  hollywood is all about second and third  and fourth chances here making that  comeback  what do you think the hollywood foreign  press association needs to do to repair  its relationships and reputation within  hollywood in order to make that come  back and become one of the most  influential award shows once again  yeah i think people have to con they  have to really think about  who they're punishing and why you know  um i'm a new member um i the 20 of the  organization is now new members um if we  meet our goals for this year it'll be  about 50 percent of the new order of the  organization that's new members um  should people like me um be punished i  mean that does that seems  counter-intuitive right um should big  winners last night have their uh wins  sullied by this i think that also seems  counterintuitive so at what point you  know are we going to move forward  really really beautifully said kimberly  i also want to talk about the industry  kind of using award shows as a great  form of advertisement for movies that  like you said you've been covering for  decades because it wasn't just nbc right  it was also netflix amazon cutting ties  with the hfpa  to what extent does the move away from  award shows potentially damage the  television and film industry as a whole  in our ability to kind of continue to go  and like see movies moving forward yeah  um i think i mean that's a fantastic  question and i think we're going to see  how that plays out i mean um  we'll see you know if absence is going  to make the heart go stronger if  hollywood really can um persevere  without you know full participation in  award shows um i think  you know the coming months are going to  tell that story but um you know  viewership has been down for the oscars  for every for many award shows um for a  while now and i think you know how we  choose to come out of covet how we  choose to deal with this going forward  um this next year is going to be  instrumental in telling that story  kimberly really appreciate your joining  us and giving us uh your perspective on  the golden globes kimberly is one of the  new members of the hollywood foreign  press association  have a great day
 

